#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171767 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "2b0e57b0-a667-5bb2-bb1e-69c4d39b799e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8474" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]?$\n\n- $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\n- $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f: X \\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $\\mathbb{R},$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[ 0, 1 ],$ the interval from $0$ and $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[ 0, 1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ but not including $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171768 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "686f5d13-dfe9-5434-abff-052c7a4b76ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8475" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\n- $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $\\mathbb Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171769 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/id #uuid "c02bc2d0-fccb-5fbe-8914-c42ebc7bb9fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8476" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\n- $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $g(x)=x-x^3$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x-x^3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x \\mapsto x-x^3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171770 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/id #uuid "086ea590-785a-539d-8b0f-b69ac7301ce4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8487" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\n- The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$ to the set $\\mathbb{R}.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4128809-363c-51c1-8592-9f3936fee831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302278} {:answer/id #uuid "5227df3f-ab60-55c7-8ac0-fe9bd93c6a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302279} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4c0902-125d-54ff-84ad-a0c0be98ff01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302280} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b2a44f-9cde-5878-9ef7-9bcd35563794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302281} {:answer/id #uuid "e110387c-5d06-579a-8c09-819eb852b13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e110387c-5d06-579a-8c09-819eb852b13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302282} :answer-field/id #uuid "6611876c-4df0-5aa8-80a6-e2e64a557ce0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f04f32d-b52c-5a8d-8938-51184b8d7e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-104778" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain ${0,1}?$\n\n- $p:{0,1}\\to Z$\n- $q:[0,1]\\to {0,1}$\n- $r:{0,1}\\to (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is ${0,1},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only.\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $(0,1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (excluding both $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2903990c-fd4a-5d91-8ede-4de1d7bc5151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302285} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ed7ee9-7247-5e2f-9f96-ea40fceed33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302286} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8b4596-b747-5cfe-907a-09e801147241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302287} {:answer/id #uuid "2adf4bca-529f-5c5d-a0a8-1f0a2c2620f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302288} {:answer/id #uuid "03267076-3d3b-5fbe-abb4-e49ff47e30ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d8b4596-b747-5cfe-907a-09e801147241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302287} :answer-field/id #uuid "b23f50a5-ebb6-5140-b58f-738dce4e9b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79ad1836-637c-5f60-ba4b-8156899fd58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104780" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $Z?$\n\n- $p:Z\\to [0,3]$\n- $q:(0,7)\\to Z$\n- $r:{Z}^{+}\\to Z$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $(0,7),$ the interval from $0$ to $7$ (excluding $0$ and $7$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is ${Z}^{+},$ the set of positive integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57372209-b4fc-57c3-bd68-b4d674865369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374299} {:answer/id #uuid "8caf0aa7-91d8-5148-a790-ec43ff6e0e25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374300} {:answer/id #uuid "40aad76c-bc42-5740-91a7-abfd42184d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374301} {:answer/id #uuid "18c53033-2638-59e1-b3be-4dab028d240e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374302} {:answer/id #uuid "7311f114-19a8-587c-bdd1-9c2299515dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c53033-2638-59e1-b3be-4dab028d240e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374302} :answer-field/id #uuid "1751ae7c-09bc-52a4-b280-12b5074667b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fd3104e-7952-590e-9c92-6b60840f5744" :question/math-academy-id "q-104781" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $[-2,2]?$\n\n- $p:[-2,2]\\to (-2,2)$\n- $r:[-2,2]\\to {-2,2}$\n- $q:Z\\to [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $[-2,2],$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $-2$ and $2$).\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $[-2,2],$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $-2$ and $2$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4ccf3c4-e40d-5f70-b574-4b14a830ee21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:X\\to [-1,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374306} {:answer/id #uuid "1be77528-bafb-5c5b-a8c8-add6d0676af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:X\\to (-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374307} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4012f9-1769-5de2-a64b-811f52871652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:X\\to {-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374308} {:answer/id #uuid "06ab703f-4cd9-53d1-b174-4a36a0242d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:(-1,1)\\to X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374309} {:answer/id #uuid "77d3d5ef-97c9-5fd5-997d-2b8259c10fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:{-1,1}\\to X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4012f9-1769-5de2-a64b-811f52871652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:X\\to {-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374308} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ca94a29-ab70-561d-930a-d1e939aaa8bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f567fe98-e0a6-523f-b099-d0ddfe44fb1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104827" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set containing the two numbers $-1$ and $1$ only.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set containing the two numbers $-1$ and $1$ only\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:X\\to {-1,1}$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $X,$ and the codomain of $g$ is ${-1,1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10831a1b-999d-56dc-831b-2ea6d0ecf1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302320} {:answer/id #uuid "2585c9a8-9e83-5943-a8e7-c052a5d0ff57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302321} {:answer/id #uuid "098ee2ea-a5a7-51fe-96d8-cc258b0ea243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302322} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2acc40-be97-5657-89dc-4959bdf8230b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302323} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a62494-1636-5846-8b9b-a048932e39e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3a62494-1636-5846-8b9b-a048932e39e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302324} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f14f18-6b7a-566f-b335-004ee8c31295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09774502-a6ed-5ac8-b6fb-e517d1f4974f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104946" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:Q\\to R?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $R$\n- The codomain of $g$ is $R$\n- The range of $g$ is $Q$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng:Q\\to R\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $Q$ to the set $R.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $Q,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $R.$ Note that $Q⊂R$ is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12756fea-d511-5413-ba42-e73f9ba0c031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: Q → X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171290} {:answer/id #uuid "2a18c8bc-4ef6-50fd-a7fb-41e818cc22c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: X → R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171291} {:answer/id #uuid "3809dc43-4135-5464-91b6-25b44f1ab772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: R → R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171292} {:answer/id #uuid "43760b88-aeaa-5fdb-b186-992ef13e2b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: X → Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171293} {:answer/id #uuid "59cf8c2b-0ce0-5a3c-a0a7-b3fdd9e769b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: R → X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a18c8bc-4ef6-50fd-a7fb-41e818cc22c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: X → R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171291} :answer-field/id #uuid "920e367e-2ddb-534f-b346-fd7c372957ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25726fd8-78e9-529e-acdf-5c4c5a237b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-96040" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of real numbers.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of real numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:X\\to R$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $X,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "891e1782-3b93-5e8e-822b-b629f3e070e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171320} {:answer/id #uuid "de2354fe-0361-598a-b082-bc2fc971dee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171321} {:answer/id #uuid "e539bf8b-d296-52ed-b3ee-d8f5447a5c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171322} {:answer/id #uuid "1effad45-a4a0-5cc5-9b28-f80d727ca50f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171323} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4f67d9-6962-56d5-a953-57aea0b11114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de2354fe-0361-598a-b082-bc2fc971dee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171321} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce0cdfe-3402-5428-9e0d-62cdd0cfa80f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06b8a099-20b6-59c6-83ca-7950b58f1030" :question/math-academy-id "q-96123" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072015/assets/dcee145044fe79dc7f4d07c3c4c1248df358cd8eda7a173cd8d4641f13404122.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $f$ is ${0,1,2,3}$\n- The codomain of $f$ is ${0,-1}$\n- The range of $f$ is ${0,-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X={0,1,2,3},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y={0,-1,-2},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is ${0,-1},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c3688c-9d19-5d7e-9716-32f69082b4d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374313} {:answer/id #uuid "72075cc6-f88f-5bf1-b8a3-b062facf0ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374314} {:answer/id #uuid "906a3a6e-08bd-562f-a581-6d4915e1b06b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374315} {:answer/id #uuid "e6385c35-931f-526a-bd2e-8fec6b5582d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374316} {:answer/id #uuid "345e4c54-127d-5a03-a09c-b856848e95a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72075cc6-f88f-5bf1-b8a3-b062facf0ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374314} :answer-field/id #uuid "461cf0d0-2a7e-5549-ab75-7d61c7e55c1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1a7d705-1868-5fb9-b847-dd44e4a77dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96744" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain $(-2,2]?$\n\n- $p:(-2,2]\\to (-2,2]$\n- $q:R\\to [-2,2)$\n- $r:[-2,2)\\to (-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $(-2,2],$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $2$ but not including $-2$).\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[-2,2),$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $-2$ but not including $2$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $(-2,2],$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $2$ but not including $-2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b739880c-ec73-5a01-9008-1d48bce8aefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302341} {:answer/id #uuid "4f561fe7-0ccd-582b-91aa-8436c7371012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302342} {:answer/id #uuid "56531bc0-1108-5803-9c33-6252b8095882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302343} {:answer/id #uuid "97a6076b-51af-56c2-ab83-405722ca1198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302344} {:answer/id #uuid "d977fc8d-ff36-5d85-b825-69fe3dd01309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56531bc0-1108-5803-9c33-6252b8095882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302343} :answer-field/id #uuid "596e1808-c1f4-5bc3-9e92-2d2f5cb6cfd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "002be73b-0850-5dcf-98e6-fc20bcccc216" :question/math-academy-id "q-96745" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain ${1,2}?$\n\n- $p:{1,2}\\to R$\n- $q:R\\to [1,2)$\n- $r:[1,2)\\to {1,2}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is ${1,2},$ the set containing the two numbers $1$ and $2$ only.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $[1,2),$ the interval from $1$ to $2$ (including $1$ but not including $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df67ba0a-4b43-53f0-9bdf-c272367ee29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302355} {:answer/id #uuid "03d79efc-c602-5870-9f6a-ad72010a0e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302356} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6a120a-62ff-599a-9577-58fee624934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Y\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302357} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cf0d57-57ae-5d89-910c-6b13b1dfec62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Y\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302358} {:answer/id #uuid "cb582515-c081-5d16-9e87-97cd3d8f9913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Q\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df67ba0a-4b43-53f0-9bdf-c272367ee29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302355} :answer-field/id #uuid "af6b5e7d-f5a9-5349-a289-7ef6f6628e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "017b78ef-16f0-5056-9e83-f2f23609a4ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-96752" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set $Y.$\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $f:R\\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $f$ is $R,$ and the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32885433-3ff6-55fd-929d-4cf8cfa93713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302362} {:answer/id #uuid "86a0f1d6-b455-58ac-87b6-04e2e0682b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:[0,1]\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302363} {:answer/id #uuid "408e9659-9975-590b-92a7-8989d5bfe97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302364} {:answer/id #uuid "63bc92d9-b927-50ca-9c06-10f6e2abeac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302365} {:answer/id #uuid "57f3b777-d1ba-55c1-bb0c-6b364645149e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "408e9659-9975-590b-92a7-8989d5bfe97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302364} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c460148-b14c-519b-93b9-48b692f533df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8741fc43-1918-5d52-9141-b25d4ba5dd79" :question/math-academy-id "q-96755" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set of real numbers.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set of real numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:Q\\to R$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6cfb481-1a1a-5995-b36f-704788f2cb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: {-1, 1} → Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171386} {:answer/id #uuid "1f72b295-5dc6-5de9-a440-6a166bad61eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: Y → {-1, 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171387} {:answer/id #uuid "42d33ac1-91a0-540f-b9e3-ce712fa996fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: (-1, 1) → Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171388} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc5d8a4-bdd6-51e2-80c7-1203a5cd0874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: Y → (-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171389} {:answer/id #uuid "7eac8757-f801-5ecd-841e-e5230ff09196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: [- 1, 1] → Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d33ac1-91a0-540f-b9e3-ce712fa996fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: (-1, 1) → Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171388} :answer-field/id #uuid "d748f4c0-474e-5edd-8bf3-3f9ecbb40c3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43abe7b9-a529-5979-a0bc-e71f6d23d3cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96758" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $f$ maps the interval from $-1$ to $1$ exclusive to the set $Y.$\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $f$ maps the interval from $-1$ to $1$ exclusive to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $f:(-1,1)\\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $f$ is $(-1,1),$ and the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ebe3765-c71f-522e-bba7-1f0ed9399cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:-x↦3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374320} {:answer/id #uuid "aa724212-8213-5c9e-a19f-1ccbf55e0e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:x-3{x}^{2}↦x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374321} {:answer/id #uuid "015208f2-3039-5743-b1e9-acbab534acc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:-x↦x-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374322} {:answer/id #uuid "8b167d77-93bd-5716-8d5e-fd572fedc5c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:x↦3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374323} {:answer/id #uuid "97f8e3f5-6751-5fb3-bbaa-569fc6adabcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:x↦x-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97f8e3f5-6751-5fb3-bbaa-569fc6adabcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:x↦x-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374324} :answer-field/id #uuid "98560b4d-b3e7-54fe-a8da-b6c553e2317d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e244f75d-631d-5045-bff6-f3fa9f9b44d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96815" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-3{x}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $g(x)=x-3{x}^{2}$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x-3{x}^{2}$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x↦x-